{"id":74,"course_id":19,"title":"Timeline, Communications, and Mitigation","summary":"Keep users and responders aligned while reducing blast radius.","sequence":2,"body":"## Mental model\nDuring incidents, communication is part of mitigation. A timeline records what changed and when. Mitigation choices should stop the bleeding while preserving evidence and avoiding uncoordinated production changes.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice writing stakeholder updates that are honest about impact, action, uncertainty, next update time, and owner without dumping raw debugging details.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nProduce a live incident document with status, impact, suspected trigger, actions taken, commands run, decisions, owners, and handoff notes.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-incident-response-reliability","course_category":"Incident Response","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":366,"term":"Incident Response and Reliability Leadership","context":"Incident Response","definition":"Practice incident command, severity, mitigation, communications, timelines, postmortems, corrective actions, and game days for platform roles."},{"id":367,"term":"Evidence","context":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":368,"term":"Blast radius","context":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":369,"term":"Rollback","context":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":370,"term":"Runbook","context":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":220,"lesson_id":74,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Timeline, Communications, and Mitigation?","answer":"Keep users and responders aligned while reducing blast radius.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":221,"lesson_id":74,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":222,"lesson_id":74,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
